Package: pmsampsize
Version: 2.0.0
Title: Sample Size Calculations for Clinical Prediction Models
Authors@R: c(person("Joie", "Ensor", role = c("aut", "cre"), email = "j.ensor@bham.ac.uk"))
Maintainer: Joie Ensor <j.ensor@bham.ac.uk>
Description: Computes the minimum sample size required to develop or
    externally validate a clinical prediction model. pmsampsize() gives the
    minimum sample size for developing a model with a continuous, binary or
    time-to-event outcome, using the criteria of Riley et al. (2019)
    <doi:10.1002/sim.7993>, Riley et al. (2019) <doi:10.1002/sim.7992> and
    Riley et al. (2020) <doi:10.1136/bmj.m441>; for binary outcomes the
    anticipated Cox-Snell R-squared can be approximated from a reported
    C-statistic (Riley et al. (2021) <doi:10.1002/sim.8806>).
    pmvalsampsize() gives the minimum sample size for the external validation
    of an existing model with a binary outcome, using the criteria of Riley et
    al. (2021) <doi:10.1002/sim.9025> and Archer et al. (2021)
    <doi:10.1002/sim.8766>. The pmgen_*() functions implement the general,
    simulation-based sample size framework of Riley et al. (2026)
    <doi:10.1186/s12874-026-02856-7> for binary outcomes: development datasets are
    simulated at each candidate sample size, the model is fitted with any
    chosen method (e.g. logistic regression, penalised regression or a
    user-supplied fitting function), and the distribution of its performance
    in the target population is estimated, giving the minimum sample size that
    meets user-specified criteria.
